,"latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported (Amended) by the Yeas and Nays: 40 - 2.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Civil actions and liability","Government employee pay, benefits, personnel management","Health care costs and insurance","Judicial review and appeals","Prescription drugs","User charges and fees"],"sponsorBioguideId":"A000148","cosponsorCount":39,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/6610","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Pharmacy benefit managers, which this bill targets with new liability and judicial review, are typically part of or contracted by health insurers managing federal employee drug benefits."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill directly affects pharmacists and the terms under which they dispense prescription drugs, altering the contractual and fee structure in the federal employee drug supply chain."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The bill creates new federal civil causes of action and judicial review procedures, which would generate litigation between pharmacists and PBMs."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would allow pharmacists to sue pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs) in federal court if they believe PBM fees or contract terms are unfair, and would let courts review and potentially overturn those fees. The bill applies specifically to prescription drug plans offered to federal employees and retirees. It creates a new legal pathway for pharmacists to challenge the business practices of the middlemen who manage drug benefits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who fills a prescription. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.05366184557252676},"overlapCount":40},"meaning":{"percentile":67.78671714781511,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6518130155197976}},{"billId":"119-hr-6617","bioguideId":"G000581","cycle":2026,"score":0.004998306833355316,"matches":[{"industry":"education","donorAmount":23500,"donorShare":0.008944338543898986,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.004998306833355316}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6518130155197976},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-6617","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"6617","title":"Keeping All Students Safe Act","introducedDate":"2025-12-11","latestActionDate":"2025-12-11","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Education and Workforce, and in addition to the Committee on Armed Services, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Education","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"B001292","cosponsorCount":47,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/6617","industries":[{"industry":"education","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly regulates schools and Head Start programs, imposing new training, compliance, and procedural obligations on educational institutions."},{"industry":"government","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"State educational agencies must establish and enforce new policies, and federal agencies administer grants and withhold payments for noncompliance."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would prohibit schools and Head Start programs that receive federal funding from confining students alone in locked rooms (seclusion) and would restrict the use of physical restraints to situations where a student poses an imminent danger of serious physical injury. It requires staff training in crisis intervention, establishes reporting procedures after restraint incidents, and allows students to sue if they are unlawfully restrained or secluded. States must create enforcement policies, and federal agencies can withhold payments from programs that violate the rules; a grant program would help states implement these requirements.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Students, teachers, and the families paying for it.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S001145","score":0.029809990260422435},"overlapCount":45},"meaning":{"percentile":23.59993689856444,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6518130155197976}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b759595d68f3a8d502","billId":"119-hr-7008","date":"2026-07-22T16:51:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll280.xml"},{"id":"2afa499bcaceefdcecfa862a","billId":"119-hr-8800","date":"2026-07-22T16:37:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll278.xml"},{"id":"e69a4fe10a0cc4e4d35ffc3d","billId":"119-hr-6955","date":"2026-07-21T22:05:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll271.xml"},{"id":"c68077c5b6dd6fe8eb42bf43","billId":"119-hres-1438","date":"2026-07-21T14:39:00-04:00","question":"Recorded Vote","result":"Passed","position":"No","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll254.xml"},{"id":"3944446cfd7ceaa28fbc0515","billId":"119-hres-1438","date":"2026-07-21T14:11:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Nay","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll253.xml"},{"id":"7d8b32832c29b7b8b63de5d9","billId":"119-hr-1118","date":"2026-07-21T14:03:00-04:00","question":"2/3 Yea-And-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll252.xml"},{"id":"a1e6877fc617d20fe7bb5ae7","billId":"11